concept("cdc-at-scale", "de", "CDC at scale: snapshots, ordering and schema drift", ADV, "A",
  "cdc at scale debezium initial snapshot incremental snapshot signal table chunks log sequence number lsn ordering per key kafka partition tombstones replication slot wal schema drift ddl heartbeat",
  "Running change data capture on large, busy databases raises three problems: taking the first full copy without stopping the source, keeping changes in order for each row, and coping when tables change shape.",
  "A first snapshot of a 2 TB table can take hours, and the log keeps moving meanwhile. Incremental snapshots, as in Debezium, copy the table in primary-key chunks while streaming continues, and resolve clashes between chunk rows and live changes. Ordering is guaranteed only per key: partition Kafka topics by primary key, carry the log position (LSN in PostgreSQL, SCN in Oracle), and have the sink apply a change only if it is newer than the stored one. Deletes arrive as delete events plus tombstones for compaction. Schema drift, such as a new or renamed column, should flow through a schema registry with compatibility rules, and breaking DDL should alert someone. Watch the source too: a stalled consumer makes a PostgreSQL replication slot keep WAL until the disk fills, so monitor lag and send heartbeats.",
  "A 3 TB orders table is snapshotted in 50,000-row chunks over 9 hours while 4,000 changes a second keep streaming. The sink applies an update only when its LSN is higher than the stored one, so late retries never overwrite newer rows.",
  "Why can't you rely on events for different primary keys arriving in the order they happened at the source?",
  "Senior interviews ask how you take a first snapshot without downtime, keep per-key order and idempotent applies, handle deletes and DDL, and stop replication slots from filling the source disk.",
  ["cdc", "schema-registry", "exactly-once-e2e"]),
concept("mcp-integrations", "ds", "Connecting LLMs to tools and data with MCP", GA, "I",
  "model context protocol mcp server client host tools resources prompts stdio streamable http oauth authorization tool poisoning prompt injection least privilege connector agent integration",
  "The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open standard for connecting AI applications to tools and data. You write a server once, and any MCP-aware app or agent can use its tools without custom glue code.",
  "Anthropic released MCP in 2024 and it is now an open project under the Linux Foundation. An MCP host, such as a chat app or IDE, runs clients that talk to servers. A server offers tools (functions the model can call, each with a JSON schema), resources (data the app can read, such as files or tables) and prompts (reusable templates). Local servers run over stdio; remote ones use Streamable HTTP with OAuth for authorisation. The 2026-07-28 version of the spec made the core stateless, so any server instance behind a load balancer can answer any request, and moved long-running tasks into an extension. For data teams, an MCP server can expose a warehouse, a catalogue or a metrics layer. Treat every tool result as untrusted: it can carry prompt injection. Use read-only, least-privilege credentials and ask the user before actions that change data.",
  "A team writes one MCP server with three tools: search_tables, describe_table and run_readonly_sql capped at 10,000 rows. Their IDE assistant and chat app both use it, and every query runs as a read-only service account.",
  "What is the difference between an MCP tool and an MCP resource?",
  "Interviewers check that you know the host, client and server roles, the three primitives, local versus remote transports and the security risks: prompt injection, over-broad credentials and unapproved writes.",
  ["function-calling", "llm-agents", "data-apis"]),
